Assume every morphism of $\mathcal C$ is monic. For a representable presheaf $F=\mathcal C(-,A)$, the category $(1\downarrow F)$ is the <category of elements>, equivalently the slice $\mathcal C/A$. A morphism from $f:B\to A$ to $g:C\to A$ is an $h:B\to C$ satisfying $gh=f$. Since $g$ is monic, there is at most one such $h$. Thus $(1\downarrow F)$ is a <preorder>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Assume every category of elements of a representable presheaf is a preorder. Let
$$
P=\coprod_{A\in\operatorname{ob}\mathcal C}(1\downarrow\mathcal C(-,A)).
$$
A disjoint union of preorders is a preorder. The category-of-elements projection $P\to\mathcal C$ is a <discrete fibration>. It is surjective on objects because $B\in\mathcal C$ is the image of the object $1_B:B\to B$ in the summand indexed by $B$.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Suppose a preorder $P$ admits a discrete fibration $q:P\to\mathcal C$ that is surjective on objects. Given $f:B\to A$ in $\mathcal C$, choose $a\in P$ above $A$. The discrete-fibration property lifts $f$ to $\widetilde f:b\to a$. Every arrow in a preorder is monic, and a <discrete fibration> preserves monomorphisms by part (b), so $f=q(\widetilde f)$ is monic. This proves the remaining implication and hence the equivalence.

